# Phase & Feature Delivery Process

When implementing any Phase or Feature in Hatli, follow this order. Do **not** jump to Compose screens or ViewModels before product/data contracts are clear.

## Execution order (mandatory)

1. Product Analysis
2. UX Flow
3. Navigation
4. Data Model
5. Firestore Collections
6. Domain Models
7. DTOs
8. Repository Interfaces
9. Repository Implementations
10. UseCases
11. ViewModels
12. UI State
13. Compose Screens
14. Components
15. Validation
16. Error Handling
17. Loading
18. Empty State
19. Testing
20. Code Review
21. Refactor
22. Documentation

## Feature isolation rule

- Finish **one feature completely** (through Definition of Done) before starting the next.
- Never leave a half-wired feature and switch to another feature "in parallel" unless the user explicitly asks.

## Required Phase artifacts

Before / during each Phase, produce or update (under `docs/phases/`):

| Artifact | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| Product Requirements | What problem are we solving? |
| Scope | In / Out |
| User Flows | Happy path + edge paths |
| Data Models | Domain entities & relationships |
| Architecture Decisions | Why this design; trade-offs |
| Screen Specifications | Screens, states, actions |
| API / Firebase Design | Collections, paths, auth rules notes |
| Test Cases | Manual + automated targets |
| Definition of Done | Binary checklist |
| Code Review Checklist | Review gates |

## Architecture constraints (from Phase 0)

- Features never depend on other features — only on `core/*`.
- No Firebase / Room / Retrofit in UI or domain.
- UI → ViewModel → UseCase → Repository → Data sources.
- MVVM + UDF (`UiState` / `UiEvent` / `UiEffect`).
- Design system components only in feature UI (no raw Material buttons in features).

## When the user starts a new Phase

1. Start at **Product Analysis** — do not code screens first.
2. Confirm Scope (In/Out) with the user if ambiguous.
3. Walk UX Flow + Navigation before writing repositories.
4. Implement data → domain → presentation in that order.
5. Close with Testing, Review, Refactor, Documentation.
